        "SIGNALMENT": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "SIGNALMENT",
          "canonical_heading": "SIGNALMENT",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Dog and cat.",
              "pdf_page": 1127,
              "printed_page": 1045,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "German shepherd dog, Golden retriever (phimosis)—observed congenital preputial stenosis; possibly hereditary.",
              "pdf_page": 1127,
              "printed_page": 1045,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Siamese cat—overrepresented.",
              "pdf_page": 1127,
              "printed_page": 1045,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "SIGNS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "SIGNS",
          "canonical_heading": "SIGNS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Phimosis—can go undetected until unsuccessful copulation attempts as an adult; severe cases in the neonate resulting in preputial urine pooling may lead to balanoposthitis and septicemia.",
              "pdf_page": 1127,
              "printed_page": 1045,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Paraphimosis—initially: licking of an exteriorized penis; after hours of exposure: ischemic necrosis and urethral obstruction; edema and swelling may make differentiation from priapism difficult.",
              "pdf_page": 1127,
              "printed_page": 1045,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Priapism—persistent penile erection lasting >4 hours; bulbus glandis firm and swollen.",
              "pdf_page": 1127,
              "printed_page": 1045,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "CAUSES": {
          "present": false,
          "status": "missing",
          "source_heading": null,
          "canonical_heading": "CAUSES",
          "content_blocks": []
        },
        "RISK FACTORS": {
          "present": false,
          "status": "missing",
          "source_heading": null,
          "canonical_heading": "RISK FACTORS",
          "content_blocks": []
        },
        "CAUSES & RISK FACTORS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "CAUSES & RISK FACTORS",
          "canonical_heading": "CAUSES & RISK FACTORS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Phimosis—abnormally small preputial orifice; congenital or acquired (e.g., caused by injury or disease); may be associated with persistent penile or preputial frenulum (thin band of connective tissue joining the penis and prepuce along the ventral glans).",
              "pdf_page": 1127,
              "printed_page": 1045,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Paraphimosis—preputial hair rings; stenotic preputial orifice; injury; os penis fractures; neurologic disease (encephalomyelitis, intervertebral disc disease); penile swelling (neoplasia, balanoposthitis, strangulation with foreign body); incompetent preputial muscles.",
              "pdf_page": 1127,
              "printed_page": 1045,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Priapism—ischemic (most common and serious in nature, veno-occlusive/low flow): cause often unknown, trauma during mating, chronic distemper encephalomyelitis, penile thromboembolism, amphetamine use, penile neoplasia, perineal abscess; nonischemic (arterial/high flow): trauma, vasoactive drugs, neurologic conditions, canine distemper; less likely to cause necrosis due to lack of venous obstruction.",
              "pdf_page": 1127,
              "printed_page": 1045,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    },
    "DIAGNOSIS": {
      "present": true,
      "status": "content",
      "content_blocks": [],
      "subsections": {
        "DIFFERENTIAL DIAGNOSIS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "DIFFERENTIAL DIAGNOSIS",
          "canonical_heading": "DIFFERENTIAL DIAGNOSIS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Phimosis (cats)—inability for penis to exteriorize in prepubertal tom cats is normal",
              "pdf_page": 1127,
              "printed_page": 1045,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "as penile adhesion breakdown is androgendependent and occurs about 7–12 months of age; early neutered cats may continue to maintain penile adhesion.",
              "pdf_page": 1127,
              "printed_page": 1045,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Paraphimosis—abnormality of the retractor penis muscles or prepuce muscles, large preputial opening, short prepuce, or priapism.",
              "pdf_page": 1127,
              "printed_page": 1045,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Priapism—urethral obstruction.",
              "pdf_page": 1127,
              "printed_page": 1045,
              "column": 2
            }
          ]
        },
        "CBC/BIOCHEMISTRY/URINALYSIS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "CBC/BIOCHEMISTRY/URINALYSIS",
          "canonical_heading": "CBC/BIOCHEMISTRY/URINALYSIS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Usually normal.",
              "pdf_page": 1127,
              "printed_page": 1045,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Phimosis in neonates—septicemia may result from preputial urine pooling.",
              "pdf_page": 1127,
              "printed_page": 1045,
              "column": 2
            }
          ]
        },
